WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.430
Today, many members of the YouTube math community
are getting together to make videos about their favorite numbers

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:16.260
over 1,000,000, and we're encouraging you - the viewers to do the same. Take a look at the description for details.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:21.120
My own choice is considerably larger than a
1,000,000, roughly 8×10^53.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.680
For a sense of scale, that’s around the
number of atoms in the planet Jupiter, so it might seem

00:00:25.689 --> 00:00:30.480
completely arbitrary, but what I love is that
if you were to talk with an alien civilization

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.940
or a super-intelligent AI that invented math
for itself without any connection to our particular

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:41.440
culture or experiences, I think both would
agree this number is something very peculiar and that it

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.700
reflects something fundamental.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:44.800
What is it, exactly?

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.300
Well, it’s the size of the monster, but
to explain what that means, we're gonna need to back up

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:52.820
and talk about group theory.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:56.820
This field is all about codifying the idea
of symmetry.

00:00:56.830 --> 00:01:03.101
For example, when we say a face is symmetric,
what we mean, is that you can reflect it about a line, and

00:01:03.101 --> 00:01:08.330
it’s left looking completely the same; it’s
a statement about an action that you can take.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:13.811
Something like a snowflake is also symmetric, but in
more ways. You can rotate it 60 degrees, or

00:01:13.811 --> 00:01:19.320
120 degrees, you can flip it along various different
axes, and all these actions leave it

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:20.950
looking the same.

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:27.020
A collection of all of the actions like this taken
together is called a “group”. kind of... at least...

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:31.440
groups are typically defined more abstractly
than this, but we’ll get to that later.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.740
Take note, the fact that mathematicians have co-opted
such an otherwise generic word for this seemingly

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:42.620
specific kind of collection should give you some
sense how fundamental they find it. Also take note;

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:48.259
we always consider the action of doing nothing
to be part of a group, so if we include that do-nothing

00:01:48.259 --> 00:01:52.670
action, the group of symmetries of a snowflake
includes 12 distinct actions.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:56.549
It even has a fancy name, “D6”.

00:01:56.549 --> 00:02:01.229
The simple group of symmetries that only has two elements acting on a face also has a fancy

00:02:01.229 --> 00:02:03.499
name, “C2”.

00:02:03.499 --> 00:02:08.080
In general there’s a whole zoo of groups,
with no shortage of jargon to their names,

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.480
categorizing the many different ways that something
can be symmetric.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.720
When we describe these sorts of actions, there’s
always an implicit structure being preserved.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:22.100
For example, there are 24 rotations that I can apply to a cube that leave it looking the

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:27.460
same, and those 24 actions taken together do indeed constitute a group.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:31.980
But if we allow for reflections, which is kind
of a way of saying the orientation of the

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:36.120
cube is not part of its structure we intend to preserve, you get a bigger group,

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.460
with 48 actions in total.

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:42.280
If you loosen things further and consider the faces to be a little less rigidly attached,

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:44.800
maybe free to rotate and get shuffled around,

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:48.060
you would get a much larger set of actions.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:52.220
And yes, you could consider these symmetries in the sense that they leave it looking the same,

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:55.740
and all these shuffling rotating actions
do constitute a group,

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:59.140
but it's a much bigger and more complicated group.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:06.080
The large size in this group reflects the
much looser sense of structure which each action

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:09.000
preserves.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.299
The loosest sense of structure is if we have
a collection of points and consider any way

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:18.040
that you could shuffle them, any permutation, to be a
symmetry of those points.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:22.820
Unconstrained by any underlying property that needs to be preserved, these permutation groups can

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:24.000
get quite large.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.140
Here, it's kind of fun to flash through every possible permutation of 6 objects and see how many there are.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:45.360
In total, it amounts to 6!, or 720.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.520
By contrast, if we gave these points some structure, maybe making them the corners of a hexagon

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:52.820
and only considering the permutaions that preserve

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:56.940
how far apart each one is from the other, we get only the 12 snowflake

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:59.989
symmetries we saw earlier.

00:03:59.989 --> 00:04:05.439
Bump the number of points up to 12, and the
number of permutations grows to about 479

00:04:05.439 --> 00:04:06.670
million.

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:10.550
The monster that we’ll get to is rather
large, but it’s important to understand

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:15.400
that largeness in and of itself isn’t all
that interesting with groups; the permutation

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:16.799
groups already make that easy to see.

00:04:16.799 --> 00:04:22.630
If we were shuffling 101 objects,
for example, with the 101 factorial different

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:29.050
actions that can do this, we have a group with a size of around 9×10^159.

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:34.690
If every atom in the observable universe had a copy of that universe inside itself, this is

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:37.920
roughly how many sub-atoms there would be.

00:04:38.229 --> 00:04:44.000
These permutation groups go by the name S sub n, and they play an important role in group theory.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.480
In a certain sense, they encompass all other
groups.

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:51.920
And so far you might be thinking:  “ok, this intellectually playful enough, but is any of this

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:53.520
actually useful?”

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.360
One of the earliest applications of group
theory came when mathematicians realized that

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.880
the structure of these permutation groups
tells us something about solutions to polynomial

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:04.530
equations.

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:08.650
You know how in order to find the two roots
of a quadratic equation, everyone learns a

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:10.840
certain formula in school?

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:15.500
Slightly lesser known is the fact that there’s also
a cubic formula, one that involves nesting cube

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:17.400
roots with square roots in a larger expression.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:23.360
There’s even a quartic formula for degree
4 polynomial, which is an absolute mess.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:26.480
It's almost impossible to write without factoring things out.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:31.560
And for the longest time mathematicians struggled
to find a formula to solve degree 5 polynomials.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:34.900
I mean, maybe there's one, but it's just super complicated.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:40.280
It turns out though, if you think about the group
which permutes the roots of such a polynomial,

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:44.490
there’s something about the nature of this
group that reveals that no quintic formula

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:46.020
can exist.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:50.380
For example, the 5 roots of the polynomial
you see on screen now, they have definite values,

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:52.680
you can write out decimal approximations,

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:58.380
but what you can never do, is write those exact values
by starting with the coefficients of the polynomial

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:02.600
and using only the four basic operations of arithmetic together with radicals,

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:04.600
no matter how many times you nest them.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:09.260
And that impossibility has everything to do
with the inner structure of the permutation

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:11.540
group S5.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:17.759
A theme in math through the last two centuries
has been that the nature of symmetry in and

00:06:17.759 --> 00:06:23.509
of itself can show us all sorts of non-obvious
facts about the other objects we that we study.

00:06:23.509 --> 00:06:27.860
To give just a hint of one of the many many ways
this applies to physics, there’s a beautiful

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:32.670
fact known as Noether’s theorem saying that
every conservation law corresponds to some

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:35.060
kind of symmetry, a certain group.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:39.700
So all these fundamental laws like conservation
of momentum and conservation of energy

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:41.740
each correspond to a group.

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:48.060
More specifically, the actions we should be able to apply to a setup, such that the laws of physics don't change.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:51.960
All of this is to say that groups really are
fundamental, and the one thing I want you

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:56.300
to recognize right now is that they’re one
of the most natural things that you could study.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:06:58.460
What could be more universal than symmetry?

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:03.220
So you might think the patterns among groups
themselves would somehow be very beautiful

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:04.550
and symmetric.

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:07.199
The monster, however, tells a different story.

00:07:07.199 --> 00:07:10.650
Before we get to the monster, though, at this
point some mathematicians might complain that

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:16.050
what I’ve described so far are not groups,
exactly, but group actions; and the groups are

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:18.280
something slightly more abstract.

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:22.340
By way of analogy, if I mention the number
“3”, you probably don’t think about

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:27.960
a specific triplet of things, you probably
think about 3 as an object in and of itself, an

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:30.759
abstraction, maybe represented with a common symbol.

00:07:30.759 --> 00:07:34.809
In much the same way, when mathematicians
discuss the elements of a group, they don’t

00:07:34.809 --> 00:07:39.099
necessarily think about specific actions on
a specific object, they might think of these

00:07:39.099 --> 00:07:44.169
elements as a kind of thing in and of itself, maybe
represented with symbols.

00:07:44.169 --> 00:07:48.889
For something like the number three, the abstract
symbol does us very little good unless we define

00:07:48.889 --> 00:07:54.300
its relation with other numbers, for example
the way it adds and multiplies with them.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:59.410
For each of these, you could think of a literal
triplet of something, but again most of us

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:03.949
are comfortable, probably even more comfortable,
using the symbols alone.

00:08:03.949 --> 00:08:09.360
Similarly, what makes a group a group are
all the ways that its elements combine with each

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:10.240
other.

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:14.340
And in the context of actions, this has a very vivid meaning; what we mean by combining

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:16.900
is to apply one action after the other,

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:22.300
read from right to left; if you flip a snowflake
about the x-axis, then rotate it 60 degrees

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:28.840
counterclockwise, the overall action is the
same as if you had flipped it about this diagonal line.

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:35.600
All possible ways that you could combine two elements of a group like this

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:37.840
defines a kind of multiplication.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:41.040
That is what really give a group its structure.

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:45.260
Here, I’m drawing out the full 8x8 table of the symmetries of a square.

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:50.240
If you apply an action on the top row and follow it 
by an action from the left column, it’ll be

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:54.740
the same as the action in the corresponding grid
square.

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:04.420
But if we replace each of these symmetric actions with something purely symbolic, well, the multiplication

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:09.160
table still captures the inner structure of the
group, but now it’s abstracted away from

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:14.860
any specific object it might act on, like
a square, or roots of a polynomial.

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:19.780
This is entirely analogous to how the usual
multiplication table is written symbolically,

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:23.120
which abstracts away from the idea of literal
counts.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:28.380
Literal counts, arguably, would make it clearer what’s
going on, but since grade school we all grow

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:30.340
comfortable with the symbols.

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:34.760
After all, they’re less cumbersome, they free
us up to think about more complicated numbers,

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:37.140
and they also free us to think about numbers in new and very different ways.

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:42.820
All of this is true of groups as well, which are best understood, as abstractions.

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:46.100
above the idea of symmetry actions.

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:50.500
I’m emphasizing this for two reasons, one
is that understanding what groups really are

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:52.940
gives a better appreciation for the monster.

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:57.660
And the other is that many students learning about
groups for the first time can find them frustratingly

00:09:57.660 --> 00:09:59.529
opaque, I know I did.

00:09:59.529 --> 00:10:05.930
A typical course starts with this very formal and abstract definition, which is that a group is a set – any

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:10.700
collection of things – with a binary operation
– a notion of multiplication between those

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:16.900
things – such that this multiplication
satisfies four special rules, or axioms.

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:22.060
And all this can feel, well, kind of random,
especially when it isn’t made clear that

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:27.589
all these axioms arise from the things that must be obviously true when you’re thinking about actions

00:10:27.589 --> 00:10:29.240
and composing them.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:34.940
To any students among you with such a course in the future, I would say if you
appreciate that the relationship groups have

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:39.730
with symmetric actions is analogous to the
relationship numbers have with counts, it

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:42.220
can help to keep a lot of the course a lot more grounded.

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:46.440
An example might help to see why this kind
of abstraction is desirable.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:51.540
Consider the symmetries of a cube and the
permutation group of 4 objects.

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:56.040
At first, these groups feel very different;
you might think of the one on the left as

00:10:56.040 --> 00:11:00.800
acting on the 8 corners in a way that preserves
the distance and orientation structure among them,

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:05.700
but on the right we have a completely
unconstrained set of actions on a much smaller

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:07.500
set of points.

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:12.120
As it happens, though, these two groups are
really the same, in the sense that their multiplication

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:14.340
tables will look identical.

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:18.720
Anything that you can say about one group will
be true of the other.

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:23.810
For example, there are 8 distinct permutations
where applying it three times in a row gets

00:11:23.810 --> 00:11:28.320
you back to where you started (not counting
the identity). These are the ones that cycle

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:31.280
three elements together.

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:38.300
There are also 8 rotations of a cube that
have this property, the various 120 degree rotations

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:40.240
about each diagonal.

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:42.660
This is no coincidence.

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:47.170
The way to phrase this precisely is to say
there’s a one-to-one mapping between rotations

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:52.480
of a cube and permutation of four elements
which preserves composition.

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:58.920
For example, rotating 180 degrees about
the y-axis, followed by 180 about the x-axis, gives

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:04.240
the same overall effect as rotating 180 degree around the z-axis; remember, that’s

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:06.760
what we mean by a product of two actions.

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:12.560
And if you look at the corresponding permutations, under a certain one-to-one association,
this product will still be true,

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:14.700
applying the two actions on the left

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:18.540
gives the same overall effect as
the one on the right.

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:22.980
When you have a correspondence where this remains true for all products, it’s called an

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:27.700
“isomorphism”, which is maybe the most
important idea in group theory.

00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:33.020
This particular isomorphism between cube rotations
and permutations of four objects is a bit

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:37.220
subtle, but for the curious among you, you
may enjoy taking a moment to think hard about

00:12:37.220 --> 00:12:41.820
how the rotations of a cube permute its four
diagonals.

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:50.480
In your mathematical life, you'll see more examples of a given group arising from seemingly unrelated situations,

00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:54.280
and as you do, you’ll get a better sense for what group theory is all about.

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:58.760
Think about how a number like 3 is not really about a particular triplet of things, it’s about

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:01.139
all possible triplets of things.

00:13:01.139 --> 00:13:05.400
In the same way a group is not really about
symmetries of a particular object, it’s

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:09.000
an abstract way that things even can be symmetric.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:12.560
There are even plenty of situations where
groups come up in a way that doesn’t feel

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:17.329
like a set of symmetric actions at all, just
as numbers can do a lot more than count.

00:13:17.329 --> 00:13:22.279
In fact, seeing the same group come out of
different situations is a great way to reveal

00:13:22.279 --> 00:13:28.290
unexpected connections between distinct objects;
that’s a very common theme in modern math.

00:13:28.290 --> 00:13:31.900
And once you understand this about groups,
it leads you to a natural question, which

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:35.680
will eventually lead us to the monster: What
are all the groups?

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:39.920
But now you’re in a position to ask that question in a more sophisticated way: What

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:44.500
are all the groups up to isomorphism, which
is to say we consider two groups to be the

00:13:44.500 --> 00:13:49.580
same if there’s an isomorphism between them.

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:53.820
This is asking something more fundamental
than what are all the symmetric things, it’s

00:13:53.829 --> 00:13:58.029
a way of asking what are all of the ways that
something can be symmetric?

00:13:58.029 --> 00:14:01.350
Is there some formula or procedure for producing
them all?

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:06.320
Some meta-pattern lying at the heart of symmetry
itself?

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:08.800
This question turns out to be hard.

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:10.259
Exceedingly hard.

00:14:10.259 --> 00:14:15.170
For one thing, there's the division between infinite groups, for example the ones describing the symmetries

00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:19.421
of a line or a circle, and finite groups,
like all the ones we’ve looked at up to

00:14:19.421 --> 00:14:20.660
this point.

00:14:20.660 --> 00:14:25.060
To maintain some hope of sanity, let’s limit
our view to finite groups.

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:29.670
In much the same way that numbers can be broken
down in their prime factorizations, or molecules

00:14:29.670 --> 00:14:34.100
can be described based on the atoms within
them, there's a certain way that finite groups

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:38.139
can be broken down into a kind of composition of smaller groups.

00:14:38.139 --> 00:14:42.750
The ones which can’t be broken down any
further, analogous to prime numbers or atoms,

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:45.250
are known as the “simple groups”.

00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:49.600
To give a hint for why this is useful, remember
how we said that group theory can be used to prove that

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:54.149
there’s no formula for a degree-5 polynomial
the way there is for quadratic equations?

00:14:54.149 --> 00:15:00.170
Well, if you're wondering what that proof actually looks like, it involves showing that if there were some kind of mythical quintic

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:04.900
formula, something which uses only radicals
and the basic arithmetic operations,

00:15:04.900 --> 00:15:11.120
it would imply that the permutation group on 5 elements decomposes into a special kind of simple group,

00:15:11.139 --> 00:15:14.550
known fancifully as cyclic groups of prime
order.

00:15:14.550 --> 00:15:20.140
But the actual way that it breaks down involves a different kind of simple group, a different kind of atom,

00:15:20.140 --> 00:15:24.600
one which polynomial solutions built up from radicals
would never allow.

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:28.880
That is a super high level description, of
course, with about a semester’s worth of details

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:33.050
missing, but the point is that you have this
really non-obvious fact about a different

00:15:33.050 --> 00:15:37.000
part of math whose solution comes down to
finding the atomic structure of a certain

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:37.840
group.

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:41.860
This is one of many different examples where understanding the nature of these simple groups,

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:45.560
these atoms, actually matters outside of group theory.

00:15:45.560 --> 00:15:49.490
The task of categorizing all finite groups breaking down into two steps:

00:15:49.490 --> 00:15:55.020
One - find all the simple groups, and two - 
find all the ways to combine them.

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:58.970
The first question is like finding the periodic
table, and the second is a bit like doing

00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:01.260
all of chemistry thereafter.

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:05.819
The good news is that mathematicians have
found all the finite simple groups..

00:16:05.819 --> 00:16:10.600
Well, more pertinent is that they proved that the ones that they found are, in fact, all the

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:12.020
ones out there.

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:17.020
It took many decades, tens of thousands of
dense pages of advanced math, hundreds of

00:16:17.029 --> 00:16:21.800
some of the smartest minds out there, and
significant help from computers, but by 2004

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:26.880
with a culminating 12,000 pages to tie
up the loose ends, there was a definitive answer.

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:30.840
Many experts agree: this is one of
the most monumental achievements in the history

00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:33.180
of math. (sigh)

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:36.460
The bad news, though, is that this answer is absurd.

00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:41.120
There are 18 distinct infinite families of
simple groups, which makes it really tempting

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:46.060
to lean into the periodic table analogy; but
groups are stranger than chemistry because

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:51.420
there are also 26 simple groups that are just left
over that don’t fit the other patterns.

00:16:51.420 --> 00:16:54.340
These 26 are known as the sporadic groups.

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:59.200
That a field of study rooted in symmetry itself
has such a patched together fundamental structure

00:16:59.209 --> 00:17:02.070
is... I mean it’s just bizarre!

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:05.209
It’s like the universe was designed by committee.

00:17:05.209 --> 00:17:10.980
If you’re wondering what we mean by an infinite
family, examples might help: one such family of simple groups includes

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:16.690
all the cyclic groups with prime order; these
are essentially the symmetries of a regular polygon with

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:21.510
a prime number of sides, but where you’re not allowed to flip the polygon over.

00:17:21.510 --> 00:17:26.050
Another of these infinite families is very
similar to the permutation groups that we saw earlier,

00:17:26.050 --> 00:17:31.730
but there’s the tiniest constraint on how
they're allowed to shuffle n items.

00:17:31.730 --> 00:17:37.540
If they act on 5 or more elements, these groups
are simple, which incidentally is heavily

00:17:37.540 --> 00:17:41.690
related to why polynomials with degree 5 or
more have solutions that can’t be

00:17:41.690 --> 00:17:44.130
written down using radicals.

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:49.100
The others 16 families are notably more complicated, and I’m told that there’s at least a little ambiguity

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:52.840
in how to organize them into cleanly distinct
families without overlap.

00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:58.760
But everyone agrees is that the 26 sporadic groups
stand out as something very different.

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:04.750
The largest of these sporadic groups is known, thanks to John Conway, as the monster group, and its size is the

00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:06.750
number I mentioned at the start.

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:11.800
The second largest, and I promise this isn’t
a joke, is known as the baby monster group.
[monster: don't talk to me or my son ever again]

00:18:11.800 --> 00:18:18.052
Together with the baby monster 19 of the sporadic groups are in a certain sense children of the monster,

00:18:18.052 --> 00:18:20.800
and Robert Griess called these 20 the “happy
family”.

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:25.660
He called the other 6, which don’t even
fit that pattern, the pariahs.

00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:29.740
As if to compensate for how complicated the
underlying math here is, the experts really

00:18:29.740 --> 00:18:32.420
let loose on their whimsy while naming things.

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:36.440
Let me emphasize, having a group which
is big is not that big of a deal.

00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:40.290
But the idea that the fundamental building
blocks for one of the most fundamental ideas

00:18:40.290 --> 00:18:46.400
in math come in this collection that just
abruptly stops around 8×10^53?

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:48.420
That’s weird.

00:18:48.420 --> 00:18:53.480
Now, at this point, that I introduced groups as
symmetries, a collection of actions, you might

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:56.380
wonder what it is that the monster acts on.

00:18:56.380 --> 00:18:59.660
What object does it describe the symmetries of?

00:18:59.660 --> 00:19:03.460
Well, there is an answer, but it doesn’t fit into
2 or 3 dimensions to draw.

00:19:03.460 --> 00:19:09.260
Nor does it fit into 4 or 5, instead to see
what the monster acts on we’d have to jump

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:17.120
up to... wait for it... 196,883 dimensions.

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:22.040
Just describing one of the elements of this
group takes around 4 gigabytes of data, even

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:26.760
though plenty of groups that are way bigger have a
much smaller computational descriptions.

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:31.650
The permutation group on 101 elements was,
if you’ll recall, dramatically bigger, but

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:38.260
we could describe each element with very little
data, for example a list of 100 numbers.

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:47.260
No one really understands why the sporadic
groups, and the monster in particular, are

00:19:47.270 --> 00:19:48.500
there.

00:19:48.500 --> 00:19:52.070
Maybe in a few decades, there will be a clearer
answer, maybe one of you will come up with

00:19:52.070 --> 00:19:55.661
it, but despite knowing that they’re deeply
fundamental to math, and

00:19:55.661 --> 00:19:59.800
arguably to physics as well, a lot about them remains
mysterious.

00:19:59.800 --> 00:20:04.640
In the 1970s, mathematician John McKay was
making a switch to studying group theory to

00:20:04.640 --> 00:20:10.960
an adjacent field, and he noticed that a number very similar to this 196,883

00:20:10.960 --> 00:20:14.970
showed up in a completely unrelated context, or at least, almost.

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:19.900
A number one bigger than this was in the series expansion of a fundamental function in a totally

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:24.690
different part of math, relevant to these
things called modular forms and elliptic functions.

00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:28.950
Assuming that this was more than a coincidence
seemed crazy, enough that it was playfully

00:20:28.950 --> 00:20:33.680
deemed “moonshine” by John Conway, but
after more numerical coincidences like this

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:38.060
were noticed, it gave rise to what became
known as the monstrous moonshine conjecture.

00:20:38.060 --> 00:20:40.400
Whimsical names just don’t stop.

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:45.620
This was proved by Richard Borcherds in 1992,
solidifying a connection between very different

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:48.840
parts of math that at first glance seemed
crazy.

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:52.840
6 years later, by the way, he won the Fields
Medal, in part for the significance of this

00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:53.980
proof.

00:20:53.980 --> 00:20:58.140
And related to this moonshine is the connections between the monster and string theory.

00:20:58.140 --> 00:21:01.660
Maybe it shouldn’t come as a surprise that something
that arises from symmetry itself

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:06.160
is relevant to physics, but in light of just how
random the monster seems at first glance,

00:21:06.160 --> 00:21:09.640
the connection still elicits a double-take.

00:21:09.640 --> 00:21:14.660
To me, the monster and its absurd size is
a nice reminder that fundamental objects are

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:16.820
not necessarily simple.

00:21:16.820 --> 00:21:20.620
The universe doesn’t really care if its
final answers look clean; they are what they

00:21:20.620 --> 00:21:26.640
are by logical necessity, with no concern
over how easily we’ll be able to understand them.

